</node>
<node id="Intelligence Information Time">
  <data key="d0">Intelligence Information Time</data>
  <data key="d1">data</data>
  <data key="d2">Intelligence information time is a marking element that indicates when the intelligence information was collected or generated.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-5cc378900bb6beaa0154051505b51ce1</data>
  <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
  <data key="d5">1778766746</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Intelligence Accuracy">
  <data key="d0">Intelligence Accuracy</data>
  <data key="d1">data</data>
  <data key="d2">Intelligence accuracy is a marking element that indicates the precision and reliability of the intelligence information.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-5cc378900bb6beaa0154051505b51ce1</data>
  <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
  <data key="d5">1778766746</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Processing Unit And Responsible Person">
  <data key="d0">Processing Unit And Responsible Person</data>
  <data key="d1">organization</data>
  <data key="d2">Processing unit and responsible person are marking elements that identify which unit and person processed the intelligence product.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-5cc378900bb6beaa0154051505b51ce1</data>
  <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
  <data key="d5">1778766746</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Product Validity Period">
  <data key="d0">Product Validity Period</data>
  <data key="d1">data</data>
  <data key="d2">Product validity period is a marking element that indicates the time window during which the intelligence product remains valid and useful.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-5cc378900bb6beaa0154051505b51ce1</data>
  <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
  <data key="d5">1778766746</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Requirement Number">
  <data key="d0">Requirement Number</data>
  <data key="d1">data</data>
  <data key="d2">Requirement number is a marking element that links the intelligence product to its corresponding original requirement.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-5cc378900bb6beaa0154051505b51ce1</data>
  <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
  <data key="d5">1778766746</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Requirement Satisfaction Feedback Receipt">
  <data key="d0">Requirement Satisfaction Feedback Receipt</data>
  <data key="d1">data</data>
  <data key="d2">Requirement satisfaction feedback receipt is the confirmation returned by requirement units indicating whether their intelligence needs were met.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-5cc378900bb6beaa0154051505b51ce1</data>
  <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
  <data key="d5">1778766746</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Reverse Design Method">
  <data key="d0">Reverse Design Method</data>
  <data key="d1">method</data>
  <data key="d2">Reverse Design Method is a military combat planning approach that starts from combat objectives or effects and reversely conceives and designs combat actions.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-594e94fdab189bb399757a7389261e90</data>
  <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
  <data key="d5">1778766746</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Effects-Based Operations">
  <data key="d0">Effects-Based Operations</data>
  <data key="d1">method</data>
  <data key="d2">Effects-Based Operations (EBO) is a method evolved from target-based and purpose/strategy-to-task analysis methods, proposed by David Deptula for military planning.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-594e94fdab189bb399757a7389261e90</data>
  <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
  <data key="d5">1778766746</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="David Deptula">
  <data key="d0">David Deptula</data>
  <data key="d1">person</data>
  <data key="d2">David Deptula is a United States Air Force general who proposed the Effects-Based Operations method.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-594e94fdab189bb399757a7389261e90</data>
  <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
  <data key="d5">1778766746</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="United States Air Force">
  <data key="d0">United States Air Force</data>
  <data key="d1">organization</data>
  <data key="d2">The United States Air Force is the military branch where David Deptula served and which developed EBO methodology.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-594e94fdab189bb399757a7389261e90</data>
  <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
  <data key="d5">1778766746</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Effects-Based Operations White Paper">
  <data key="d0">Effects-Based Operations White Paper</data>
  <data key="d1">content</data>
  <data key="d2">The Effects-Based Operations White Paper is a document officially published by the U.S. military in 2001.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-594e94fdab189bb399757a7389261e90</data>
  <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
  <data key="d5">1778766746</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Iraq War">
  <data key="d0">Iraq War</data>
  <data key="d1">event</data>
  <data key="d2">The Iraq War in 2003 was successfully planned using Effects-Based Operations theory.&lt;SEP&gt;The Iraq War is a conflict where the US military extensively used Tomahawk cruise missiles.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-594e94fdab189bb399757a7389261e90&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-bda4e71c2e2aedaec198d4b1d87ca5b4</data>
  <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
  <data key="d5">1778766746</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Joint Operations Outline">
  <data key="d0">Joint Operations Outline</data>
  <data key="d1">content</data>
  <data key="d2">The Joint Operations Outline is a U.S. military document that added "effects" as a campaign planning element in 2006.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-594e94fdab189bb399757a7389261e90</data>
  <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
  <data key="d5">1778766746</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="ENAR">
  <data key="d0">ENAR</data>
  <data key="d1">concept</data>
  <data key="d2">ENAR (Effects-Node-Action-Resource) is a linkage method in Effects-Based Operations that studies the relationships between nodes, actions, resources, and direct/indirect effects.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-594e94fdab189bb399757a7389261e90</data>
  <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
  <data key="d5">1778766747</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="U.S. Military">
  <data key="d0">U.S. Military</data>
  <data key="d1">organization</data>
  <data key="d2">The U.S. Military is the armed forces that developed and utilizes the Global Command and Control System and Common Operating Picture for operational command and planning.&lt;SEP&gt;The U.S. Military is the armed forces that adopted Effects-Based Operations and published related doctrinal documents.&lt;SEP&gt;The U.S. Military refers to the armed forces that term the air tasking allocation cycle as the Air Combat Mission Assignment Cycle.&lt;SEP&gt;The U.S. Military is the organization to which strategist Baldwin belongs.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-594e94fdab189bb399757a7389261e90&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-67849e0b5328f68ea35509bd6b19645b&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-4e076ff2b14def31d05c594776649eee&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-2a0d1b6a8aabc98d09750deaec7618bb</data>
  <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
  <data key="d5">1778766747</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Air Operations Center">
  <data key="d0">Air Operations Center</data>
  <data key="d1">location</data>
  <data key="d2">The Air Operations Center is a command facility where combat planning tools are widely used to evaluate precision-guided weapon effects on targets.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-594e94fdab189bb399757a7389261e90</data>
  <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
  <data key="d5">1778766747</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Precision-Guided Weapons">
  <data key="d0">Precision-Guided Weapons</data>
  <data key="d1">artifact</data>
  <data key="d2">Precision-Guided Weapons are advanced munitions used by the U.S. military for precise targeting in combat operations.&lt;SEP&gt;Precision-guided weapons are advanced munitions that require target intelligence and battlefield environment information for effective use.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-594e94fdab189bb399757a7389261e90&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-4e076ff2b14def31d05c594776649eee</data>
  <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
  <data key="d5">1778766747</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Effects-Node-Action-Resource Method">
  <data key="d0">Effects-Node-Action-Resource Method</data>
  <data key="d1">method</data>
  <data key="d2">The Effects-Node-Action-Resource Method is a coordinated approach that analyzes relationships between nodes, actions, resource requirements, and effects in military planning.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-594e94fdab189bb399757a7389261e90</data>
  <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
  <data key="d5">1778766747</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Purpose Effect Analysis">
  <data key="d0">Purpose Effect Analysis</data>
  <data key="d1">concept</data>
  <data key="d2">Purpose Effect Analysis is the first step in Reverse Design Method planning, analyzing combat objectives or desired effects.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-594e94fdab189bb399757a7389261e90</data>
  <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
  <data key="d5">1778766747</data>
